const faq = [
  { q: 'Do ravens have good memory?', a: 'Ravens have excellent memory across multiple domains. Spatial memory: ravens can remember the locations of hundreds or thousands of food caches simultaneously, returning to retrieve them days, weeks, or months later. Individual recognition: ravens remember individual conspecifics and humans by face and voice, and retain this memory for years. Social events: ravens appear to remember past social interactions and use this information to predict others\' likely behaviour. Cache competition memory: ravens remember which individuals watched them cache food and prioritise recaching in locations those individuals cannot have seen.' },
  { q: 'What is object permanence in ravens?', a: 'Object permanence is the understanding that objects continue to exist even when they cannot be seen. Ravens demonstrate full object permanence — they will search behind barriers for hidden objects, follow invisible displacements (tracking an object moved behind a screen and then moved again while hidden), and understand that an object moved out of sight is still somewhere specific rather than gone. This level of object permanence (stage 6 in Piaget\'s scale) was for a long time thought to be a primate-specific ability; corvids demonstrate equivalent capacity.' },
  { q: 'Can ravens remember things from the past?', a: 'Ravens show what researchers call "episodic-like memory" — the ability to recall the what, where, and when of specific past events. This is distinct from simple conditioning (knowing a location has food) and involves representing specific past events. The best-documented example is caching memory: ravens not only remember where they cached food (where) and what food was stored (what), but also appear to track how long ago they cached it (when) — avoiding caches of perishable food after they would have spoiled. This episodic-like temporal sensitivity was described in corvids by Clayton & Dickinson (1998) and subsequently extended to ravens.' },
];
